---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CELLS } from '../../data/framework';

// The four journey pages arrive as their drafts land (Phase 2 in
// docs/plans/project-setup.md). This page ships now because the framework page
// links to it and a link to nothing is worse than a short page.
---
<BaseLayout
  title="The toolkit"
  description="Building a content operating model: what changes, what to borrow from devops, where you are now, and what to design. In development, arriving with version 1.0."
  markdownTwin="/toolkit.md"
>
  <section class="c-marketing c-marketing-section">
    <div class="c-section__inner">
      <p class="c-eyebrow">The toolkit</p>
      <h1 class="display-heading type-h1">Helping you build one</h1>
      <p class="type-intro">
        The framework says what a content operating model needs to have. The
        toolkit is about building yours, and it is being written now.
      </p>
      <ul class="c-bullets c-bullets--accent">
        <li><strong>Transforming.</strong> Why this is a change project more than an editorial one, and the roles and skills an organisation may need more and less of.</li>
        <li><strong>Concepts to borrow.</strong> What content functions can take from devops, and from the last round of digital transformation.</li>
        <li><strong>Diagnosis.</strong> Where you are now, starting with five questions worth asking in a board meeting.</li>
        <li><strong>Design.</strong> Given what you know, what to write down: a template for each of the {CELLS.length} cells.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Until then, <a href="/framework">the framework</a> is the specification,
        and the <a href="https://contentmaturity.com">Content Maturity assessment</a>
        measures the system that produces your content in about twenty minutes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
